hi everyone welcome back we hope you had  a good break and we're able to enjoy  some of the snacks in your basket  uh we're really looking forward to four  talks from different researchers now  across groups in msr that cover a range  of different topics  and  each prese each  researcher will be talking for about 20  minutes  you can put questions in the chat  channel  which the researchers  will be able to respond to uh  during uh the talks but also we'll have  a q a session at the end  where you we can we'll have a live uh  question and answer with all four  speakers  so the first of our speakers is uh  deputy  and he's from the rl group in msr and  we'll be talking about efficient  neural architecture search  so passing over you over to you  now  good morning may everyone i'm very  excited to be here talking at the ai  breakthroughs event welcome my name is  devadiptaday and i am a researcher in  the reinforcement learning group which  is part of the machine learning research  area in msr redmond i am very excited to  be here today talking to you about  a hot topic in machine learning called  neural architecture search and our  latest algorithm  this is joint work with ctelsa  hanzang who john lankford rich caravana  and eric horowitz  before we dive in i will take a moment  to introduce myself i graduated in 2015  from the robotics institute at cmu and  my background is at the intersection of  vision planning and reinforcement  learning and controls  my journey in this topic started when i  was visiting john lankford at msn new  york about two years ago and we were  somehow talking about densely and i put  up the densenet architecture table on  the projector john immediately replied  that he had no idea how these numbers  came about and what to do about it to be  honest i had no clue either  and this is the usual black magic  involved in selecting an architecture  event today there is this process of  trial and error trying several different  architectures choosing magic hyper  parameters like learning rate learning  grade schedule  initialization which can be very  frustrating and also makes it appear  that only groups with access to large  compute can complete  not only is this a frustrating process  we also have massive machine learning  talent shortage in industry today  all these factors have led the community  to focus on neural architecture search  where the goal is for computers to do  this tedious task instead of us humans  before we launch into the details of a  specific method i would like to present  a very short overview of a rapidly  expanding subfield so this will be a  five-minute literature overview of over  500 papers  being published but don't worry  every nas algorithm out there has these  three components there is a search space  which defines the space of all  realizable architectures a search  strategy to efficiently explore in this  space and often coupled with this search  strategy is some performance estimation  strategy where you're trying to figure  out what is the performance of  architectures which you haven't tried  yet  just to be clear the central challenge  in architecture search is that function  evaluation is expensive sampling a point  is expensive because a point in an  architecture point is an architecture  and to evaluate an architecture means to  go train it on the training set and find  its performance on the validation set  in literature you will find two kinds of  such spaces the first one is what is  called as the cell search space or micro  search space where it is assumed that  there exists an outer skeleton like  dense net or resnet and the search space  basically consists of finding cell  templates that will be replicated as  many times as are necessary  this has  tended to be the most widely used search  space mainly because it is much smaller  and allows some human domain knowledge  to be injected via the skeleton design  the macro search space is of course  fully general and that is what you would  generally expect  to  encounter when you think of neural  architecture search and here we don't  have any outer skeleton and cells so the  number of architectures that you can  realize is far more people tend to shy  away from this search space because it  is very large  this field is so hot that in this  excellent continuously updated list of  papers maintained by colleagues at  university of freiburg i counted more  than 200 papers in just 2020. note there  are still more than three months left if  you are interested in nas and want to  learn more about it i will recommend  starting from this excellent survey  article which is also periodically  updated by the authors  the earliest paper on nas actually goes  back to 1989 from cmu but this  particular paper by baradzoff and  quackley in 2016 really reignited the  revolution  at its core the method is very simple  there is a controller rnn which samples  architectures and sends it out to a  cluster to get trained in paragraph the  results are fed back to the controller  which updates itself so that it is more  likely to sample high performing  architectures the update rule is the  widely used reinforced algorithm the  canonical model free rl algorithm  here is a concrete example of how the  controller rnn samples an architecture  it predicts parameters of layers layer  by layer such as number of filters  filter height filter width stride height  stride bit etc while this was a great  first start it took thousands of gpu  hours to achieve reasonable results on  relatively small data sets like c410  this next paper cut down the search time  from thousands of gpu hours to a single  gpu date far more practical  on both cepha 10 and pantry bank innas  achieved good performance by using  orders of magnitude less search time  the trick that might appear barbaric at  first glance is to use weight sharing  amongst all the candidate architectures  remember the great expense in nas is  training eac