ch capacity it  needs automatically  there were a number of reasons for  growing networks as you will see shortly  it is a fully general method which can  also deal with macro search spaces it  naturally incorporates new data as they  arrive and exploits prior knowledge like  human design models which can be  especially important in production  scenarios  petri dish is inspired by gradient  boosting and directly searches the  pareto frontier or trade-off curve of  flops memory versus accuracy  petri disc is also inspired by the  earliest work on architecture search  from 1999 named cascade correlation  learning architecture  petri dish has three phases in the  beginning there is a tiny seed or parent  model from which such starts in phase  one candidate neurons are evaluated to  see if adding them permanently to the  parent model will help lower accuracy  will help  increase accuracy in phase 1 the  candidates are not allowed to affect the  original model's gradients and weights  using some tricks that i will detail  soon the candidate with the highest  weight is selected to be added to the  parent model  now let's look at phase one in a bit  more detail imagine you have a simple  model on the left you are starting out  with a candidate layer is evaluated by  adding them to the parent network using  stop forwards  solid black arrow and stop gradient  dotted black arrow lines stop forward  layer has the property that it allows  gradients to flow through but does not  allow forward activations to pass  through stop gradient has the reverse  property this allows us to accumulate  gradient  of loss with respect to the candidate  neuron without affecting the forward and  backward flow of information in the  parent model  if we decide to accept the candidate  then the stop forward and stop gradient  layers are replaced by regular  connections in phase 2.  to amortize the cost of searching for  exponentially many candidates we utilize  the same weight sharing trick you saw