isor at the university of washington  and eric horvitz and ryan white here at  microsoft  so many of the existing studies and data  sets of the kovi 19 pandemic focus on  the biomedical and  epidemiological  aspects of the case and fatality rates  including efforts in detection infection  propagation therapeutic intervention and  vaccine design  however it's important to keep in mind  that although we may not all be infected  by the coronavirus we are all affected  by the coronavirus  these challenges include societal issues  such as disparities  economic issues such as unemployment  psychosocial issues such as stress  anxiety and loneliness  and they remind us that even though  certain public health policies may be  effective in mitigating the spread of  the disease pandemic-related policy  decisions cannot be made on health  information alone  instead we need to identify and  understand the multi-level system of  human needs and the impact of the  pandemic and its associated policy  decisions on these needs in order to  inform how we can better respond to the  pandemic and better prepare for future  responses  so how do we go about understanding  human needs  a standard way to assessing needs is  through survey-based methods but they  can be costly and time-consuming to  conduct or to gain fine-grained or  real-time insights at large scales  other methods such as market research or  analyzing purchase behaviors can also  provide insights about human needs but  they're often limited to  consumer and commercial interests  e-commerce platforms such as amazon or  walmart or specialized service providers  such as talkspace for mental health or  coursera for education may have access  to large-scale real-time analysis of  user behaviors but they're often myopic  on to special needs rather than  providing a holistic view of many needs  and public data such as twitter has been  used to characterize human needs but  again they portray only subset of needs  that are externa