at maybe you will be able  to get on  a v100 with a very large network right  and uh but you may be able to see like  how much of that is acceptable to you  and trade off um in one search  alone but you you run  our hope is that you you take for your  data set you run petri dish and you come  up with a gallery of models and and then  you can uh choose which one to deploy  where without having to run search again  and again for every constraint so that  idea of directly searching the pareto  frontier is is a is a what we believe is  a very powerful thing to have in a  feature to have in the search algorithm  great thanks  um a question for alec uh so a lot of um  rl is is done in simulation uh  especially you know when you think about  safety critical scenarios you might need  to simulate those uh for certain reasons  but can you talk about uh  what you think are the sort of  challenges of moving from simulation to  the real world and and what the  differences are  limitations of simulation and advantages  of it  yeah um that's an excellent question so  uh you know  uh hit the nail right on the head uh  there's uh  obviously lots of  challenges that arise when trying to do  reinforcement learning directly in the  real world um in safety critical  settings um which is why we don't  see that as something happening uh every  day  you know and uh  certainly uh starting from simulation  and trying to do  uh simulation to reality transfer is uh  is is a natural thing to do there and  you know the one of the ways people  uh try to guard against um running into  unsafe situations there is by doing  a lot of basically perturbations to the  simulation to to make sure that the  agent is trained in environments  uh  that that that give it sort of robust  coverage against simulation errors right  so uh there are tricks called things  like domain  randomization  uh you might imagine that  opening a robotic agent in simulation  you  make sure you perturb environment  variables like