ink you know  um certainly  uh  actually one of the hardest things about  uh this extended work from home uh has  been the  uh absence of the a lot of those  serendipitous interactions where you  talk to a coworker learn about their  work and you get an idea about how  it might actually be uh immensely  beneficial to something you could do uh  but definitely uh  in my own research um and for uh most of  people i know at msr  uh we tend to be uh extremely  collaborative exactly for this reason  that um just you know having  um having inputs and ideas from a broad  group of people can be um can can be so  powerful and if certainly for  the particular uh work i went into a bit  more detail today on uh imitation  learning  so so there is there is a question that  had been bothering me really um since  2015 when i did some previous work on  imitation learning  on you know how to simultaneously  uh  how to really improve upon the  suboptimal baseline policy well and it  wasn't until we started thinking about  combining multiple baselines we landed  on a solution concept and a method  that um  did a pretty good job on solving that  challenge as well which wasn't really  something i had  started out with an idea to solve  and you know  all the credit goes to having an amazing  creative intern who came in with a  with a very determined attitude uh  uh went through the breakthroughs  experience like many of you and uh  now works in the office next to mine so  uh definitely those interactions end up  creating a lot of value for us  okay maybe i can give a  slightly complimentary perspective as  well so as iona is mentioned in the  morning at msr we are not only kind of  collaborating with all the researchers  but we also kind of talk about many of  the problems that are out in the real  world for the different products and  services that our say partner teams are  working on like the product teams  so one of the problems that we started  out or like worked on last year was a  